#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# assuming

#% text
# `assuming(facts, body)` evaluates `body` with the facts added to the assumptions, so that functions such as `simplify` and `refine` can use them.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A fact is an inequality (`x > 0`, `a <= b`), a domain fact `element(n, integers)`, or several facts joined by `and` or given as a list.

#% item
# Domains are `integers`, `rationals`, `reals`, `complexes` and their positive, negative and nonnegative parts such as `positive_reals`.

#% item
# The facts hold only while `body` is evaluated; the value of `body` is the value of the call.

#% item
# Calls of `assuming` nest: the inner facts are added to the outer ones.

#% item
# `simplify`, `refine`, `power_expand`, `limit` and the tests `is_positive`, `is_negative`, `is_nonnegative` use the assumptions. Other functions ignore them.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Without an assumption, sqrt(x^2) cannot be simplified:

#% cell
sqrt(x^2)
#% output
#= (x^2)^(1/2)

#% text
# For positive and for negative x:

#% cell
assuming(x > 0, simplify(sqrt(x^2)))
#% output
#= x

#% cell
assuming(x < 0, simplify(sqrt(x^2)))
#% output
#= -x

#% text
# An integer parameter:

#% cell
assuming(element(n, integers), refine(sin(n*pi)))
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Several facts, joined by `and` or in a list:

#% cell
assuming(x > 0 and y > 0, simplify(sqrt(x^2*y^2)))
#% output
#= x*y

#% cell
assuming([x > 0, y < 0], refine(abs(x*y)))
#% output
#= -1*x*y

#% text
# Facts that imply the sign of an expression:

#% cell
assuming(x > 2, refine(abs(x - 1)))
#% output
#= -1 + x

#% cell
assuming(x > 1, is_positive(x - 1))
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Nested calls combine their facts:

#% cell
assuming(x > 0, assuming(y > 0, refine(abs(x*y))))
#% output
#= x*y

#% text
# Logarithms:

#% cell
assuming(x > 0, simplify(log(x^2) - 2log(x)))
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
assuming(x > 0, refine(log(exp(x))))
#% output
#= x

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Simplify a solution under the conditions a model guarantees, here a positive parameter and a negative one:

#% cell
assuming(q > 0 and p < 0, simplify(abs(t /. first(solve(t^4 == q*p^2, t)))))
#% output
#= q^(1/4)*(-p)^(1/2)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `refine` with facts as a second argument does the same for one expression:

#% cell
refine(abs(x), x > 0)
#% output
#= x

#% text
# The facts are dropped after the call:

#% cell
assuming(x > 0, refine(abs(x))) + refine(abs(x))
#% output
#= x + abs(x)

#% text
# `integrate` uses them: this integral is 1/a for a > 0:

#% cell
assuming(a > 0, integrate(exp(-a*x), [x, 0, inf]))
#% output
#= a^-1

#% text
# `solve` keeps the solutions that meet the assumptions:

#% cell
assuming(x > 0, solve(x^2 == 4, x))
#% output
#= [[x -> 2]]

#% text
# Consequences for `floor` and `max`: floor(x) is 0 for 0 < x < 1, and max(a, b) is a for a > b:

#% cell
[assuming(x > 0 and x < 1, refine(floor(x))), assuming(a > b, refine(max(a, b)))]
#% output
#= [0, a]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `refine`, `simplify`, `element`, `power_expand`, `is_positive`, `integers`
